Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
> 
>  > Especially the random corridors are not really
>  > nicely put.
> 
> Well, go back with the same jaundiced eye and look
> at the corridors in the 80x21 version. They're
> equally ugly with what you've shown, which is an
> artifact both of how the corridors are drawn from
> door to door in paths following right angle turns,
> and also of how many times rooms try to connect to
> distant rooms rather than to closer rooms; the only
> difference is that on the smaller map, fewer
> uglinesses per level will occur.

Whether intended or not, this type of implementation
makes more varying and thus more interesting levels,
even if -  from a "beautify _architectural_ design"
perspective - the levels seem impractical. But this
is clearly an issue of taste. I think, that if you'd
add a "try shortest connection" like change in the
map generator then maps will look (yet more) uniform
and boring.

(I'd still like to see dynamically generated outdoor
sceneries added.)

>  > Wouldn't also object creation have to rebalanced?
>  > Otherwise you'd get a lot more objects just
>  > because there are more rooms that get random
>  > objects.
> 
> Well, yes, but you'd get them in return for exerting
> proportionally more effort. Probably the rebalancing
> would be more in terms of a lower proportion of
> exceptionally good (or bad) objects, rather than a
> change in their number.

My first thought was as well that a significant
rebalancing might be necessary. But then I got aware
that most of the interesting objects will also be
found (and used) by the strolling hostile creatures.
In this respect there's already some inherent balance.

> One thing that would need
> careful tuning would be the availability of
> foodstuffs other than monster corpses; having to
> explore farther just to find the stairs down makes
> the chance of starving greater.

Indeed. Here there may be some adjustment necessary;
though most food stems from monster corpses anyway.
But, OTOH, no one forces a player to fully explore a
level; so new strategies may just have to be applied.

Janis


